.\" Manual page for portkiller.
.TH PORTKILLER 1 "2026" "portkiller" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
portkiller \- interactive terminal port manager for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B portkiller
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B portkiller
shows who is listening on which ports, enriched with process, project,
health and kill-impact information, and stops listeners safely: host
processes get SIGTERM, then SIGKILL if still alive after 3 seconds
(\fI--force\fP sends SIGKILL straight away),
containers are stopped with docker stop or podman stop (rootless podman
containers as their owner), and systemd services through
systemctl. A PID is only signalled while its kernel start time still
matches the scan, and the start time is checked again before the SIGKILL,
so a recycled PID cannot be killed by mistake.
Running with no flags starts the interactive TUI.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-list " [" \-\-json " | " \-\-plain ]
Print a table of all listeners; \-\-json emits machine-readable JSON and
\-\-plain tab-separated fields (proto, address, port, pid, project,
process, source; "\-" for an empty field).
.TP
.BI \-\-kill " PORT..."
Stop everything listening on the given port(s). Multiple ports may be
given in one command. A container is stopped via \fIdocker stop\fP or
\fIpodman stop\fP, a
systemd unit via \fIsystemctl\fP, and a host process receives SIGTERM,
then SIGKILL if it is still alive after 3 seconds. \fI--force\fP sends
SIGKILL straight away.
A listener run by a process manager (pm2, supervisord, nodemon, forever,
runit, s6) gets a warning on stderr naming the command that stops it for
good, since the manager may start it again.
.TP
.BI \-\-kill\-name " NAME..."
Stop every listener whose process name, or container name, is
\fINAME\fP. May be combined with \fI--kill\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-restart " PORT..."
Stop the listeners on the given port(s) (or \fI--kill-name\fP), wait until
the ports are free (10 seconds unless \fI--wait\fP gives another limit),
then start the same command line again in its original directory and
environment as its original owner (with that user's supplementary
groups), logging to
\fI~/.local/state/portkiller/restart-NAME.log\fP. If the listener table
cannot be read, the ports count as still taken and nothing is relaunched.
systemd units are
restarted with \fIsystemctl restart\fP and containers with \fIstart\fP.
Nothing is stopped when a target cannot be relaunched or is owned by a
process manager. Accepts \fI--force\fP, \fI--signal\fP and \fI--dry-run\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-signal " SIG"
With \fI--kill\fP or \fI--kill-name\fP: send the one signal \fISIG\fP
(HUP, INT, QUIT, KILL, USR1, USR2, TERM, STOP, CONT, or a number) instead
of SIGTERM-then-SIGKILL. Containers get it through \fIdocker kill\fP or
\fIpodman kill\fP, systemd units through \fIsystemctl kill\fP. Cannot be
combined with \fI--force\fP.
.TP
.BR \-\-wait " [\\fISECONDS\\fP]"
After stopping, wait until the stopped ports have no listener (10 seconds
unless given) and exit 1 if one is still taken. A failed scan (\fIss\fP
not runnable) counts as taken.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
With \fI--kill\fP: print one line per listener showing what would be
stopped and how, without touching anything.
.TP
.BR \-\-free " \fISTART\-END\fP [\\fI\-\-json\fP]"
Print the first bindable TCP port in the range.
.TP
.BR \-\-history " [\\fI\-\-port\fP N] [\\fI\-\-limit\fP N] [\-\-json]"
Print recent appear/vanished events from the events log, newest first.
.TP
.BR \-\-watch " [\\fI\-\-port\fP N] [\-\-notify]"
Print port appear/vanish events and new Port Guard violations as they
happen, until interrupted. \fI--port\fP narrows the output to one port and
\fI--notify\fP also sends each event as a desktop notification. A failed
scan prints a warning and reports nothing for that pass.
.TP
.BR \-\-mcp " [\\fI\-\-allow\-kill\\fP]"
Serve the Model Context Protocol on stdin/stdout for AI agents, with the
tools list_ports, free_port, port_history, stop_port and restart_port.
Unknown tool arguments are rejected.
stop_port and restart_port only preview unless \fI--allow-kill\fP is given. Runs with the caller's
privileges and never re-executes under sudo.
.TP
.BI \-\-completions " SHELL"
Print a completion script for \fIbash\fP, \fIzsh\fP or \fIfish\fP.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help ", " \-v ", " \-\-version
Show help or version information. These, \fI--free\fP, \fI--history\fP,
\fI--completions\fP and \fI--mcp\fP run without sudo; the TUI and the
other modes re-execute themselves under sudo when it is available.
.SH KEYS
In the TUI:
.TP
.BR j / k ", arrows, " PgUp / PgDn ", " g / G
Navigate.
.TP
.BR F3 " or " /
Search (process, port, PID, address, project, branch, framework, cwd).
Esc clears the search and the marks.
.TP
.BR F4 " or " t
Cycle protocol filter TCP / UDP / ALL.
.TP
.BR F5 " or " r
Refresh (automatic every 2 seconds).
.TP
.BR F6 " or " s
Cycle sort PORT / PROCESS / PID / PROTO.
.TP
.BR F9 " or " Enter
Stop the selected listener (or every marked row the filter shows) after a confirmation that
shows its connections, clients and child processes.
.TP
.BR F8 " or " K
Force kill (SIGKILL).
.TP
.B Space
Mark or unmark the row and move down.
.TP
.BR i " or " Tab
Toggle the detail panel.
.TP
.B b
Open http://localhost:PORT in the desktop user's browser.
.TP
.BR H " / " U
Hide or unhide the process name; show hidden rows dimmed.
.TP
.BR o " / " X
Show orphans only; stop all orphans (SIGTERM).
.TP
.B h
Port history.
.TP
.B c
Check ports declared in docker-compose.yml or .env of the current
directory against running listeners.
.TP
.B P
Stop every process of the selected row's project.
.TP
.B R
Restart what the last stop took down (skips process-manager-owned ones).
.TP
.BR ? " or " F1
Help.
.TP
.BR F10 ", " q " or " Ctrl+C
Quit.
.PP
Mouse: click a row to select it, click it again or right-click to stop it,
scroll to move, click a footer key to run it.
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing stopped, at least one stop failed, a requested port or name has
no listener, a port was still taken after \fI--wait\fP, or a
\fI--restart\fP target could not be relaunched.
.TP
.B 2
Bad or unknown argument: an unknown flag, an invalid port, two commands
at once, \fI--json\fP with \fI--plain\fP, or an option without the command
it applies to.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, the TUI draws without colors and marks the cursor row with ">".
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ~/.config/portkiller/guard.conf
Guard rules, one \fIport=owner\fP per line.
.TP
.IR ~/.local/state/portkiller/events.jsonl
History log of port appear/vanish events.
.TP
.IR ~/.config/portkiller/hidden
Process names hidden from the TUI table (H toggles, U shows them).
.TP
.IR ~/.local/state/portkiller/restart-NAME.log
stdout and stderr of a process relaunched by \fI--restart\fP or R.
.PP
Under sudo these paths are in the invoking user's home and are owned by
that user. portkiller will not write through a symlink or hard link at any
of them, will not read a FIFO or device there, and replaces them
atomically. The history and restart logs are mode 0600 in a 0700 state
directory, a restart log is trimmed past 5 MiB, and none of the files is
written when no home directory can be found.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ss (8),
.BR docker (1),
.BR podman (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
